<</A 8449 0 R/S/Figure/P 6707 0 R/ActualText(The diagram consists of three columns labeled \215List of /22 Subnets\216, \215List of /23 Subnets\216, and \215List of /24 Subnets\216. In the \215/22 Subnets\216 column, two subnets are listed: 172.16.0.0/22 \(marked with a cross\) and 172.16.4.0/22 \(marked with a check mark\). The \215/23 Subnets\216 column lists four subnets, three of which \(172.16.2.0 /23, 172.16.4.0 /23, and 172.16.6.0 /23\) are marked with crosses, and one \(172.16.0.0 /23\) is highlighted at the top. The \215/24 Subnets\216 column lists eight subnets from 172.16.0.0 /24 to 172.16.7.0 /24, where subnets from 172.16.4.0 /24 to 172.16.7.0 /24 are marked with crosses, and 172.16.3.0/24 is marked with a check mark.)/Alt(The diagram consists of three columns labeled \215List of /22 Subnets\216, \215List of /23 Subnets\216, and \215List of /24 Subnets\216. In the \215/22 Subnets\216 column, two subnets are listed: 172.16.0.0/22 \(marked with a cross\) and 172.16.4.0/22 \(marked with a check mark\). The \215/23 Subnets\216 column lists four subnets, three of which \(172.16.2.0 /23, 172.16.4.0 /23, and 172.16.6.0 /23\) are marked with crosses, and one \(172.16.0.0 /23\) is highlighted at the top. The \215/24 Subnets\216 column lists eight subnets from 172.16.0.0 /24 to 172.16.7.0 /24, where subnets from 172.16.4.0 /24 to 172.16.7.0 /24 are marked with crosses, and 172.16.3.0/24 is marked with a check mark.)/T()/K 0/Pg 1076 0 R>>
endobj

7074 0 obj
<</A 8417 0 R/S/Figure/P 6707 0 R/ActualText(The network configuration includes four routers labeled R1 to R4, and a host. Each router has an I P address: R1 with 1.1.1.1, R2 with 2.2.2.2, R3 with 3.3.3.3, and R4 with 4.4.4.4. A message "Confirms my Route to 5.5.5.5" from R1 and R2 with TTL equals 2 is sent towards the host at 5.5.5.5 but gets halted. This event is marked with an \217X\220. Both R2 and R3 confirm their routes to 1.1.1.1, as indicated by a text. The final destination is the host, which has an IP address of 5.5.5.5.)/Alt(The network configuration includes four routers labeled R1 to R4, and a host. Each router has an I P address: R1 with 1.1.1.1, R2 with 2.2.2.2, R3 with 3.3.3.3, and R4 with 4.4.4.4. A message "Confirms my Route to 5.5.5.5" from R1 and R2 with TTL equals 2 is sent towards the host at 5.5.5.5 but gets halted. This event is marked with an \217X\220. Both R2 and R3 confirm their routes to 1.1.1.1, as indicated by a text. The final destination is the host, which has an IP address of 5.5.5.5.)/T()/K 0/Pg 1337 0 R>>
endobj

7075 0 obj
<</A 8388 0 R/S/Figure/P 6707 0 R/ActualText(The network routing scenario involves four routers \(R1 to R4\) and a host, each assigned with specific I P addresses \(from 1.1.1.1 to 5.5.5.5\). The process begins at R1 and R2 with a T T L of 3, intending to use a working route to reach the host at 5.5.5.5 but encounters an issue leading to the query \215Problem with Route to 5.5.5 question mark\216 at R3. A similar issue is depicted in the reverse direction where there\220s an attempt to use a working route from the host at address 5.5.5.5 back to R1 at address 1.1.1.1 but is met with \215T T L Exceeded\216 and another query \215Problem with Route to 1.1.1.1 question mark\216. )/Alt(The network routing scenario involves four routers \(R1 to R4\) and a host, each assigned with specific I P addresses \(from 1.1.1.1 to 5.5.5.5\). The process begins at R1 and R2 with a T T L of 3, intending to use a working route to reach the host at 5.5.5.5 but encounters an issue leading to the query \215Problem with Route to 5.5.5 question mark\216 at R3. A similar issue is depicted in the reverse direction where there\220s an attempt to use a working route from the host at address 5.5.5.5 back to R1 at address 1.1.1.1 but is met with \215T T L Exceeded\216 and another query \215Problem with Route to 1.1.1.1 question mark\216. )/T()/K 0/Pg 1340 0 R>>
endobj

7076 0 obj
<</A 8383 0 R/S/Figure/P 6707 0 R/ActualText(The network routing scenario involves an enterprise router \(R1\) connected to two network devices \(B01 and B02\) through ethernet W A N. The router R1 is configured to automatically obtain an IPv6 address using the command \215ipv6 address autoconfig default\216. The router then sends an N D P Router Solicitation \(R S\) message to the Internet Service Provider \(ISP1\). In response, ISP1 with subnet 2001:DB8:1:12::1/64, sends an N D P Router Advertisement \(RA\) containing the prefix \2152001:DB8:1:12::/64\216.)/Alt(The network routing scenario involves an enterprise router \(R1\) connected to two network devices \(B01 and B02\) through ethernet W A N. The router R1 is configured to automatically obtain an IPv6 address using the command \215ipv6 address autoconfig default\216. The router then sends an N D P Router Solicitation \(R S\) message to the Internet Service Provider \(ISP1\). In response, ISP1 with subnet 2001:DB8:1:12::1/64, sends an N D P Router Advertisement \(RA\) containing the prefix \2152001:DB8:1:12::/64\216.)/T()/K 1/Pg 1340 0 R>>
endobj

